## Prompt 11 — Admin Dashboard & Incident Management

1. Back up your existing database, then import `database/prompt11_admin_dashboard_migration.sql` into the current `neighbournet_db` database after Prompts 8–10. Do not re-import the base schema over an existing installation.
2. Sign in using an active account whose `users.role` is `admin`, then open `pages/admin.php`. Admin authorization is checked against the current database role and active account status on protected pages and endpoints.
3. The dashboard shows database-backed status counts, category and 14-day reporting charts, community participation, incident search/filters, evidence links, official status history, private admin notes, cluster review, comment moderation, resident account moderation and task moderation.
4. Official status changes are written to `incident_updates` and `admin_audit_logs`. Internal notes are stored in `incident_admin_notes`; report flags, comment visibility, resident account status and task status changes are also audited.
5. The Neighborhood Issue-Management Index is an operational performance metric, not a safety score. Its methodology and weights are shown in the dashboard.
6. Follow `tests/PROMPT11_ADMIN_TEST_PLAN.md` to validate workflows with an admin and resident account. The PHP syntax checks have been run; live MySQL and browser workflows must be verified in your local XAMPP environment.
